SET('p3-7','On the left are the users.',"On the left are the users. Site staff, like investigators and pharmacists, use the IRT web UI for screening, randomisation, re-randomisation, eConsent status and DARM for drug accountability. It is component-based: a legacy ASP.NET MVC portal with Angular sub-applications, and a newer React and TypeScript shell app whose features are separate npm packages, both sharing Keycloak single sign-on. Internal teams build studies in our Admin console tooling, and external partners connect through integrations. In the middle is a hybrid backend, hosted on-premise on IIS: a legacy .NET Framework 4.8 core with the transaction engine for screening, randomisation and dispensing, and newer .NET 6 to 10 APIs built with clean architecture, MediatR and FluentValidation, which call the engine through a transaction engine API. For data, each study has its own SQL Server database, plus a global master database, with Redis for caching. RabbitMQ carries events and work queues for .NET worker services, like notifications and cache refreshes, and Quartz.NET runs scheduled jobs. Integrations with EDC systems, depots and couriers run through MuleSoft, mostly as SFTP file exchanges, and are moving into .NET data transfer APIs. Across everything: correlation ids, audit trails, CI/CD through our on-premise Azure DevOps Server, and Splunk and Grafana for monitoring.")
R('p3-7','the randomisation service allocates the slot in a transaction, a kit is assigned, an event is published, and inventory and notifications react.','the transaction engine allocates the slot and assigns a kit in one transaction in the study database, then a RabbitMQ event drives notifications, and a MuleSoft flow updates the sponsor\'s EDC.')
